# Testing policy

Code here is written mostly by AI agents, so tests are the spec. A change without tests is not done.

## Every feature, bug fix or behaviour change ships tests in the same change

| Level | Where it goes | Run with |
|---|---|---|
| Rust unit / property | `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` in the changed file; `proptest` for invariants | `cargo test -p <crate>` |
| Rust HTTP handlers | same file, axum router via `tower::ServiceExt::oneshot` (fakes: `karbird-ingest/src/testing.rs`) | `cargo test -p <crate>` |
| Web / engine TS | `*.test.ts` next to the file in `apps/web/src` (vitest) | `pnpm --filter @karbird/web test` |
| SDK, demo system, scripts | `packages/sdk-node/test/`, `packages/sdk-php/test/`, `examples/demo-system/test/`, `scripts/test/` (`node --test` / `composer test`) | `just test` / `just test-php-sdk` |
| Integration (real binaries, Kafka, ClickHouse, Postgres) | `tests/integration/<area>.test.mjs`, helpers from `tests/integration/lib.mjs` | `just test-integration` |
| E2E (browser) | `tests/e2e/specs/<area>.spec.ts`, helpers from `specs/helpers.ts` (`login`, `ingestLog`, `collectErrors`) | `just test-e2e` |

Required levels by change type:
- **UI or user-visible behaviour** (page, button, map, timeline, dialog): vitest for the logic **and** a Playwright spec that clicks through it as a user and checks the visible result.
- **API route or auth/permission rule**: Rust handler test **and** an integration test for success, 401/403, CSRF and tenant isolation. If the UI uses it, an e2e spec too.
- **Ingest / processor / data flow**: Rust unit test **and** an integration test that sends real OTLP and reads the result back through the API.
- **Engine (Rust/WASM)**: unit + property tests in engine-core; an e2e spec if rendering or interaction changes.
- **Bug fix**: first a regression test that fails without the fix, at the lowest level that reproduces it, plus e2e if the user saw the bug in the browser.
- **Security-relevant code** (redaction, tokens, sessions, CSP, headers): negative tests — what must be rejected, redacted or never returned.

#### Performance measurements (Criterion, Divan, Hyperfine, Peak RSS)

- On Windows, use only the approved tools: **Criterion** for statistical function-level latency/throughput comparisons; **Divan** as a complementary microbenchmark and for allocation counts/bytes; **Hyperfine** for repeated finite command-line workloads; and **Peak RSS** from Windows process accounting (`Get-Process` `PeakWorkingSet64`) for native peak resident memory. Peak RSS is an OS measurement, not a package. Do not add or recommend other performance-measurement tools under this workflow.
- Keep Criterion and Divan in `[dev-dependencies]` and dedicated `benches/` targets only. Keep Hyperfine and RSS measurement outside the production executable. Never add benchmark allocators, profiling hooks, or measurement-only startup code to production paths.
- Every Rust package with meaningful CPU work should have at least one benchmark for representative production work; add multiple workloads when a package has distinct material algorithms. For each new or changed function, decide whether measuring it would be useful. Benchmark parsers, validation, redaction, conversion, aggregation, serialization, compression, layout, batching, and other repeated CPU work.
- Use Criterion as the primary evidence for latency changes. Use Divan when allocation count/size matters; its instrumented timing is not production-latency evidence. Use Hyperfine only for finite end-to-end commands with stable inputs and setup. Hyperfine process timings do not measure a running service's request latency. Measure child processes separately for Peak RSS when relevant; report bytes and do not substitute virtual memory or cumulative allocated bytes.
- Do not benchmark trivial getters, constant accessors, thin delegation, I/O latency, database/Kafka/network operations, or one-line glue just to increase coverage. For I/O packages, benchmark CPU-only transformation and validation paths; if none are meaningful, document why a benchmark would mislead.
- Benchmarks must call production code with representative, deterministic inputs and `black_box`; separate fixture construction from timed work unless setup is part of the user workload. Avoid randomness, I/O, mutable workloads, and concurrent heavy activity in controlled runs.
- Use `just bench` for workspace benchmark targets. For recorded comparisons, use the persistent workflows in `performance/`: `run-benchmarks.ps1` for Criterion, `run-divan-suite.ps1` for allocations, and `run-app-workloads.ps1` for Hyperfine plus Peak RSS. Keep results on the same machine and build profile; label materially different environments non-comparable. Do not make noisy shared-runner timing thresholds a required CI gate. CI should compile benchmark targets through `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets`.
- A benchmark is a measurement, not proof of optimization. Keep correctness tests, measure before and after meaningful changes, repeat noisy results, and report uncertainty and any unmeasured dimensions. Never claim coverage beyond the workloads actually measured.

## Test quality

- Assert behaviour a user or client can observe, not implementation details. Cover edge cases and error paths, not only the happy path.
- Playwright: locate by role/label/text (`getByRole`, `getByLabel`), never by CSS classes; no new fixed `waitForTimeout` — use `expect(...).toBe...` auto-waits or `expect.poll`. Integration: `eventually(...)` instead of sleeps.
- Tests are independent and repeatable: unique data (`randomHex`, `randomUUID`), no reliance on run order or on data from other tests.
- Actions allow one open plan per target and really change the demo: create as few plans as a test needs and leave the demo in its default state.
- Never delete, skip or weaken an existing test to get green. Fix the code, or fix the test only if it is provably wrong, and say so. `test.skip` only with a stated environmental reason in the code.

## Running

- While working: run only the affected crate/package/spec, trimmed to the summary (e.g. `... 2>&1 | Select-Object -Last 20`, Playwright `--reporter=line`). Read full logs only on failure.
- Before claiming done: `just check`, `just test`, and — for anything touching UI, API or data flow — `just test-integration` and `just test-e2e` with `just dev` + `just demo` running.
- If a suite could not be run, say so explicitly; never report it as passed.
